            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Urdu for to crouch out of sight, then the Urdu for to huddle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: فکر کرنا",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "فکر کرنا — fikr karnā — \"to worry\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "امی ہر بات کی فکر کرتی ہیں۔ ammī har bāt kī fikr kartī haiṅ. Mum worries about everything."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Past, with نے (he or she …): اس نے بچوں کی فکر کی, us ne bachchoṅ kī fikr kī."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "فکر کرنا (fikr karnā) is built from فکر, usually traced, through Persian, to Arabic fikr, \"thought\", and کرنا, \"to do\": in Urdu the thought has become a worry."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for describing what you lived and felt."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
